Drop AVC denial records that name a hidden type -- uid-gated.

`avc: denied { ... } scontext=u:r:ksu:s0` in the kernel log names a type no
stock policy carries, so anything that can read the log can read the kernel's
root status straight out of it.

THE UID GATE IS THE POINT, AND IT IS NEW

This was the only pair of guards in _hook with no uid gate: it deleted the whole
record class, for every task, at every uid. That is too much. The record it
deleted is the same one that diagnosed the uid-gate-widening incident written up
in common/_hook/README.md -- servicemanager at uid 1000 proxying a
`selinux_check_access("u:r:ksu:s0", ...)` and being refused once a second from
8.17s of uptime, with the previous boot's dmesg carrying zero of them. Without
that record in the log the failure looked like a userspace problem and the
kernel change that caused it was invisible.

So the filter now matches every other site in this directory: it fires at
`uid >= 2000` and nowhere below. An app-uid task's denial no longer names a root
type; a system- or root-uid task's denial still does, and stays diagnosable from
dmesg. The residual is deliberate and worth stating plainly: a reader that can
get at the kernel log can still see system-originated records naming a covered
type. That is the price of keeping the incident above debuggable, and it is why
this is a gate rather than a blanket delete.

CAVEAT: avc_audit() can run in softirq for the network checks, where `current`
is whatever task was interrupted, so the gate is not meaningful for those. It is
safe -- `current` is never NULL -- and root frameworks do not generate network
AVCs against their own types, but do not read a network denial's presence or
absence as a signal.

APPLIES TO

6.6 and 6.12, whose security_sid_to_context() no longer takes a
`struct selinux_state *`. Use quiet_selinux_audit_legacy.patch on 5.10 / 5.15 /
6.1.

READ THIS BEFORE EDITING THE BUILDER'S VARIANT TABLE. The two files have
BYTE-IDENTICAL context -- the insertion point is a blank-line-delimited spot
around `a->selinux_audit_data = &sad;`, which is the same on all five versions --
so each one dry-runs clean on all five and only fails at COMPILE time on the
wrong one. `apply_first_of` must therefore pin the variant by kernel version;
letting it take the first that applies picks the wrong file on three of the five
and turns a silent mis-selection into a build break at best.

Verified with `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` on android15-6.6 (6.6.142) and
android16-6.12 (6.12.90); security/selinux/avc.o compiles clean on both
(ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1). Generated by editing the real file and running `diff -up`.
--- a/security/selinux/avc.c
+++ b/security/selinux/avc.c
@@ -778,6 +778,48 @@ noinline int slow_avc_audit(u32 ssid, u3
 
 	a->selinux_audit_data = &sad;
 
+	/*
+	 * AID_SHELL (2000) and up: drop this record when either end names a
+	 * hidden type, matching the uid gate every other site in _hook uses.
+	 *
+	 * The gate is the point, and it is NOT symmetry for its own sake. An
+	 * ungated filter deletes the whole class -- including
+	 * "avc: denied { find } ... scontext=u:r:ksu:s0" emitted by
+	 * servicemanager at uid 1000 -- which is the exact record that
+	 * diagnosed the uid-gate-widening incident written up in
+	 * common/_hook/README.md. Below uid 2000 the record is kept, so a
+	 * system-side breakage is still diagnosable from dmesg; at and above
+	 * it, an app-uid task's denial no longer names a root type.
+	 *
+	 * Caveat worth knowing: avc_audit() can run in softirq for the network
+	 * checks, where `current` is whatever task was interrupted, so the gate
+	 * is not meaningful for those. It is safe (current is never NULL) and
+	 * root frameworks do not generate network AVCs against their own types,
+	 * but do not read a network denial's presence or absence as a signal.
+	 */
+	if (__kuid_val(current_uid()) >= 2000) {
+		u32 hidden_sids[2] = { tsid, ssid };
+		int i;
+
+		for (i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
+			char *ctx = NULL;
+			u32 len;
+			bool hidden;
+
+			if (i == 1 && ssid == tsid)
+				break;
+			if (security_sid_to_context(hidden_sids[i], &ctx, &len) || !ctx)
+				continue;
+			hidden = strnstr(ctx, ":ksu:", len) ||
+				 strnstr(ctx, ":ksu_file:", len) ||
+				 strnstr(ctx, ":lsposed_file:", len) ||
+				 strnstr(ctx, ":zygisk_file:", len);
+			kfree(ctx);
+			if (hidden)
+				return 0;
+		}
+	}
+
 	common_lsm_audit(a, avc_audit_pre_callback, avc_audit_post_callback);
 	return 0;
 }
